  \country{Country}}
\email{second@example.edu}

\begin{abstract}
Wireless learning experiments are written twice: once against a simulator, and
again --- differently --- against radios, a testbed's container contract, and
whatever ports that testbed will let through. UnionLabs removes the second
writing. An algorithm declares only \emph{what to transmit} and \emph{what to
receive}; the platform carries each payload over a real single-carrier/OFDM
modem on USRP hardware, over LoRa, or over no radio at all, with no change to
the algorithm. This demo introduces the layer added since our earlier PHY work:
the \emph{wiring of a multi-node experiment is now a file}. One JSON document
names the nodes, the radio each owns down to the connector and RF channel, the
port each listens on, the address others must dial to reach it through a
NodePort, and --- per link, per direction --- whether that hop is carried over
the air, over TCP/IP, or over LoRa. Every node reads the same file and asks it
one question: \emph{which node am I}. We demonstrate federated and
decentralised learning running unchanged across a laptop, a lab pair of USRPs,
and a shared Kubernetes-managed testbed, including a chain whose first hop is
over the air and whose second is Ethernet.
\end{abstract}

