%% ===========================================================================
\section{Motivation}
%% ===========================================================================
An experiment that puts learning inside the radio loop --- federated averaging
over the air~\cite{mcmahan2017fedavg}, decentralised consensus over a
graph~\cite{lian2017dpsgd}, over-the-air computation~\cite{nazer2007computation}
--- is easy to state and expensive to run. The algorithm is a page of code. What
surrounds it is not: a modem, an ARQ path, which USRP is on which connector,
which node dials which port, and a testbed whose containers cannot even see each
other until somebody publishes a port.

Shared platforms removed the hardware barrier~\cite{powder,colosseum,aerpaw},
and open PHY stacks removed the waveform barrier~\cite{gnuradio,srslte,oai}. The
barrier that remains is that an experiment's \emph{wiring} lives in people's
heads and in shell history. It is retyped on every machine, and the retypings
disagree --- a client dials 5700 while the server serves 5701; a node is told to
transmit on a radio that is receive-only. The failure surfaces minutes later,
from a layer that is not the one at fault.

UnionLabs makes the wiring a first-class, checkable artifact, and keeps the
algorithm ignorant of it.

%% ===========================================================================
\section{The substrate (prior work)}
%% ===========================================================================
Two pieces predate this demo and are summarised only so the new layer makes
sense.

\textbf{The PHY engine.} A C++ modem (\code{sdr\_system}) implements frame
synchronisation, carrier-frequency and phase correction, equalisation,
(de)modulation across BPSK/QPSK/8-PSK/16-QAM and their differential variants,
convolutional/LDPC/turbo coding, CRC-16, and stop-and-wait ARQ, over
single-carrier or OFDM waveforms, driving USRP N210/B210/X310 through UHD. It
is reachable in-process (as a Python extension, for radio-free runs with AWGN)
or as a separate process driving real radios.

\textbf{The union API.} An algorithm implements three methods ---
\code{produce()}, \code{consume(msg)}, and optionally \code{on\_result(ack)} ---
and declares the shape of its payload. It imports nothing from any driver. That
single rule is what lets one file run over the ideal channel, the USRP modem, or
LoRa by changing one flag, and it is enforced by a self-test that runs every
experiment over every radio-free PHY.

%% ===========================================================================
\section{What is new}
%% ===========================================================================

\subsection{The wiring is a file}
A topology is a JSON document under \code{/workspace/experiments/topologies/}.
It names each node, its role in the algorithm's own vocabulary, the radio it
owns, the ports it listens on, and the links between nodes. Each node is
launched with the same file and its own identity:

\begin{quote}\code{./run.sh -{}-algo fl -{}-topology fl-chain-mixed -{}-node n2}\end{quote}

\noindent Everything else --- role, peers, ports, device arguments, connector,
subdevice, gains --- is resolved from the file, and anything typed on the
command line still wins over it. \code{-{}-print-plan} resolves a node and
prints what it would do without running anything.

\subsection{The medium belongs to the link}
Each link states how it is carried, and the two directions may differ:
\code{\{"up": "wireless", "down": "tcp"\}}. This is not a convenience. Our rig
has one transmit-capable B210 and one receive-only N210, so the only RF path is
B210$\rightarrow$N210 and the reply \emph{must} return over TCP. A chain can
therefore change medium half way (Figure~\ref{fig:wiring}): the middle node
receives over the air and forwards over Ethernet, which is exactly what an
RX-only node in the middle can do.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=6mm,
  every node/.style={font=\small},
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=8mm, minimum width=17mm,
              align=center, font=\small\bfseries, fill=black!3},
  rf/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick, densely dashed},
  ip/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
]
\node[box] (n1) {n1\\[-1pt]\scriptsize client};
\node[box, right=16mm of n1] (n2) {n2\\[-1pt]\scriptsize relay};
\node[box, right=16mm of n2] (n3) {n3\\[-1pt]\scriptsize server};

\draw[rf] (n1) -- node[above, font=\scriptsize] {wireless}
                node[below, font=\scriptsize] {B210$\rightarrow$N210} (n2);
\draw[ip] (n2) -- node[above, font=\scriptsize] {tcp}
                node[below, font=\scriptsize] {Ethernet} (n3);

\node[below=7mm of n1, font=\scriptsize, align=center] (p1)
  {radio: \code{serial=30CD424}\\ TX/RX, A:A, 78\,dB\\ binds: --- };
\node[below=7mm of n2, font=\scriptsize, align=center] (p2)
  {radio: \code{addr=192.168.10.2}\\ RX2, A:0, 25\,dB\\ binds: ack, net};
\node[below=7mm of n3, font=\scriptsize, align=center] (p3)
  {no radio\\ \phantom{RX2, A:0}\\ binds: net};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{One file, three nodes, two media. The medium is a property of each
link and of each direction; the reply on hop one returns over TCP because the
N210 never transmits. Ports a node \emph{binds} are what a host must publish.}
\label{fig:wiring}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What the file refuses}
A wiring that cannot run is rejected when it is read, naming the node --- not
three minutes later by UHD. A wireless direction whose transmitter has no
transmit section; two nodes on one host claiming one port; peer ports that break
the \code{base+k} rule the peer transport actually follows; a misspelled key; a
link to a node that does not exist; a reply asked to travel over the air. Each
of these was a real failure with an error that named the wrong layer.

