\subsection{Running on a shared testbed}
The platform image follows the testbed's own container contract: it stays alive
and idle, and the platform execs its VNC server into it. A session hook
materialises the shared workspace, publishes what the node is (its radios,
addresses and PHYs) as a heartbeated record, and attaches the radio's network
into the session's own namespace. Because a container never receives traffic
sent to its host, each \emph{listener} must be published; a node that only
transmits publishes nothing. A NodePort renumbers the port, so a node states
both what it binds and what others should dial:

\begin{quote}\code{"ports": \{"ack": 5599\}, "advertise": \{"host": "10.10.1.23", "ports": \{"ack": 35999\}\}}\end{quote}

\subsection{Everything is checked}
The claim ``the same algorithm runs everywhere'' is only worth making if it is
tested. A single command runs every experiment over every radio-free PHY, walks
44 command-line flags and 82 topology settings from the text a user types to the
object each one configures, and launches multi-node topologies as real
processes. \TODO{update these counts at submission time.}

%% ===========================================================================
\section{Demonstration}
%% ===========================================================================
Visitors see the \emph{same} experiment file three times, and watch the wiring
change underneath it.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{No radio.} Federated averaging on MNIST, one server and two
clients as three processes on a laptop, wired by \code{fl-star-tcp}. Accuracy
climbs per round.
\item \textbf{Over the air.} The same algorithm, wired by
\code{fl-star-radio}: a B210 transmits each model over the 915\,MHz link to an
N210, which answers over TCP. Visitors watch per-chunk ARQ, retransmissions and
airtime.
\item \textbf{Mixed chain.} \code{fl-chain-mixed}: hop one over the air, hop
two over Ethernet, with the relay's own log naming each leg it opened.
\item \textbf{On the testbed.} The same commands in a remote session on a
Kubernetes-managed testbed, reached over VNC, with its N210 attached and its
ports published.
\item \textbf{Break it on purpose.} We edit the file to ask a receive-only
radio to transmit; the run refuses at load and says which node and which link.
\end{enumerate}

